{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>At that time, saying.</b> Its saying <b>לֵאמֹר</b> — I have already written in many places that wherever one does not state the very things themselves in their exact form, he says לֵאמֹר. So too in the matter before us: it is not that He commanded them at that time in this precise language, but the intent of the command was to say to them ״שָׁמֹעַ״ etc." "Or perhaps it further intends to allude to what they said, of blessed memory, in Sanhedrin (Yerushalmi Sanhedrin 3:8:7), and this is its language: Rav Huna, when he would see a meritorious matter concerning a certain person and did not know him, he would open for him from the verse ״פְּתַח פִּיךָ לְאִלֵּם״ (Proverbs 31:9) — end of quote. And this is what he alluded to in the word <b>לֵאמֹר</b>, for he must speak lest truth be lacking and he judge justly." "<b>Further,</b> it may be explained according to the way of what they said in Sanhedrin (Sanhedrin 7a), and which Rambam ruled in chapter 21 of Hilkhot Sanhedrin, and this is its language: the judge must hear the claims from the litigants and repeat their claims, as it is said (I Kings 3:23) ״וַיֹּאמֶר הַמֶּלֶךְ זֹאת אוֹמֶרֶת״ etc. And this is what is intended in the statement <b>לֵאמֹר שָׁמֹעַ</b> — meaning, that the judge must say with his own mouth what he heard from the litigants." "<b>Hear between your brothers etc.</b> It is difficult: why is there need to command this — if they do not hear, on what will they judge? Further, why did He say it in this manner and not say ״שִׁמְעוּ״? Rather, the intention is that they be persistent in hearing and not grow weary, and this matter divides into two things: the first is from the litigants themselves — if they say ״we still have more to argue, we still have more to prove,״ the judges should not grow weary of their words, and all the more so when they bring proof after proof and it did not help, the judge should not say ״again there is no proof and no claim,״ but hear with persistence. And the second is that if the litigants imposed trouble upon them, they should not say ״we have already taken much trouble today and there is no more hearing a plaintiff and defendant until after some time,״ but hear without interruption. And here they were commanded regarding the precision of the anguish of judgment." "<b>Further,</b> it intends to warn them to be discerning from within the words spoken between the disputants, to recognize the truth of the matter. For even if according to the claims the innocent party would be acquitted and the liable party held liable — if it is recognized from the order of their words and from the movement of their lips the opposite of what the law would obligate — the judge has only what his eyes see. And that is its saying <b>שָׁמֹעַ</b> in the sense of discernment; <b>בֵּין אֲחֵיכֶם</b> meaning, what passes between them of words and disputation; <b>וּשְׁפַטְתֶּם צֶדֶק</b> meaning, according to what becomes clear to you that it is just, even if it be contrary to the formal order of procedure — for judgment comes only for a judge who does not know the matter with certainty; a judge has only what his eyes see." "<b>Further</b>, it intends along the lines of what they said in tractate Makkot, Mishnah at the end of chapter 1 (Makkot 6b), and this is their language: ״that a court may not hear from the mouth of a translator,״ etc., end of quote. And this is what is meant by <b>hear between your brothers</b> — and not from the translator." "<b>Further</b>, it intends that the judge should not brighten his face and lift his eyes toward one of them while lowering his eyes from the other, but rather that the manner of hearing should be equal between them. And this is what is meant by <b>hear</b> — meaning, the manner of hearing should be <b>between your brothers</b>: if you lift your eyes, lift them toward both, and if you lower them, lower them toward both. Or it is possible that HaShem commanded not to lift his eyes at all toward them, for it is possible that one of them might deceive him through the faculty of sight, that he shows a favorable eye more than the other, and his arguments would be suppressed. And I heard from the mouth of a great sage, a pious man and great in Israel, beloved to me as my own soul — he is the Rav, Rabbi Moses Birdugo, may his memory be for the life of the World to Come — that he was precise during the time of judgment to keep his eyes downward and would not lift his eyes at all, and he would sense that if he lifted his eyes toward the necessary direction of one of the litigants, the one opposite him would become disconcerted. And this is what is meant by <b>hear between your brothers</b> — that they should do only the hearing, and the words coming from the pleaders before them without any differentiation toward either of them; and through this, ״וּשְׁפַטְתֶּם צֶדֶק״." "<b>Between a man and between his brother, etc.</b> The verse intends to warn concerning three laws that surround just judgment. The first, along the lines of what Rambam wrote in chapter 22 of Laws of the Sanhedrin, and this is his language: ״that you should not say, so-and-so… perhaps he will kill my son,״ etc., end of quote. Corresponding to this He said <b>between a man</b>, in the manner that they of blessed memory expounded (Sanhedrin 6a) on the verse ״לֹא תָגוּרוּ מִפְּנֵי אִישׁ״. When two brothers come to judgment, the judge's reasoning might lead him to say: there is no need to be precise in this judgment, for these men are brothers. Therefore He said <b>and between his brother</b>." "It also intends what they said in the chapter שבועת העדות (Shevuot 31a), and which Rambam ruled in chapter 21 of Laws of the Sanhedrin: litigants where one of them was dressed in fine garments, etc. — he says to the distinguished one, ״either dress him like yourself until you argue with him, or dress like him,״ etc., end of quote. And this is what is intended by His saying <b>and between his brother</b> — that they should be brothers at one level of attire, for otherwise just judgment will not emerge, as Rambam wrote there at the beginning of the chapter." "Third, according to what they said in the chapter שבועת העדות (Shevuot 30a), and this is their language: the Rabbans taught, ״בְּצֶדֶק תִּשְׁפֹּט עֲמִיתֶךָ״ (Leviticus 19:15) — that one should not be sitting while the other stands, etc. And this is what is meant by <b>and between his ger</b> — meaning that he should be ׳residing׳ like him, either both seated or both standing; for without this there is no just judgment here."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {2 "Citation normalized from the abbreviation סנהדרין ז: to the standard Talmudic reference Sanhedrin 7a.", 3 "עִנּוּי הַדִּין literally 'the affliction of the judgment,' a rabbinic idiom for the painstaking effort required in judicial proceedings.", 6 "The phrase רוּחִי literally means 'my spirit'; rendered as 'my own soul' to convey the idiom of deep personal endearment."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:14:44.536291855Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}